WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.656
Welcome. This learning is about making the work you

2
00:00:03.678 --> 00:00:08.033
already do more understandable, accessible and responsive to people.

3
00:00:08.611 --> 00:00:10.789
You do not need to know every culture or

4
00:00:10.811 --> 00:00:14.278
have authority over an entire program to begin. You

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.056
do need a willingness to notice, ask, and follow

6
00:00:17.078 --> 00:00:17.433
through.

7
00:00:18.209 --> 00:00:21.253
Across these lessons, we will use the National CLAS

8
00:00:21.276 --> 00:00:25.164
Standards to examine practical decisions. The name refers to

9
00:00:25.187 --> 00:00:29.520
culturally and linguistically appropriate services. The standards come from

10
00:00:29.542 --> 00:00:32.142
the United States Department of Health and Human Services

11
00:00:32.164 --> 00:00:35.231
Office of Minority Health. They provide a framework for

12
00:00:35.253 --> 00:00:38.809
organizations. Here, we will connect that framework to individual

13
00:00:38.831 --> 00:00:41.276
work within the Disability Services Division.

14
00:00:41.998 --> 00:00:45.842
This is voluntary, self-directed learning. You can pause, revisit

15
00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:49.242
an explanation, or take a break. You do not

16
00:00:49.264 --> 00:00:51.998
need a partner or a group. When you reach

17
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.176
a reflection, you can think quietly, use your own

18
00:00:55.198 --> 00:00:58.242
notes, or work with the companion sheet. You do

19
00:00:58.264 --> 00:01:02.042
not need to share personal experiences. Please leave names,

20
00:01:02.176 --> 00:01:06.042
case details, health information and other private information out

21
00:01:06.064 --> 00:01:06.976
of your learning notes.

22
00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.666
Choose one ordinary part of your work that you

23
00:01:11.688 --> 00:01:14.932
can examine safely. It might be a public notice,

24
00:01:15.288 --> 00:01:19.288
an instruction, a form, a meeting invitation, an internal

25
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.510
process, or a recommendation you help prepare. If you

26
00:01:23.532 --> 00:01:26.177
do not have an example in mind, follow the

27
00:01:26.199 --> 00:01:28.888
sample service notice that we will use throughout this

28
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:29.354
curriculum.

29
00:01:30.169 --> 00:01:32.880
Our sample notice asks a person to attend a

30
00:01:32.902 --> 00:01:36.213
service-planning conversation. It explains the date but uses unfamiliar

31
00:01:36.236 --> 00:01:40.126
acronyms. It provides a telephone number without explaining how

32
00:01:40.148 --> 00:01:43.948
to request communication assistance. Staff have noticed that some

33
00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:47.413
people do not respond. Those observations give us questions

34
00:01:47.436 --> 00:01:50.037
to explore. They do not tell us why any

35
00:01:50.059 --> 00:01:51.969
particular person did not respond.

36
00:01:52.537 --> 00:01:56.003
For your own example, write one sentence about its

37
00:01:56.026 --> 00:01:59.714
purpose. Then describe what the person receiving it needs

38
00:01:59.737 --> 00:02:02.759
to be able to do. Those are different questions.

39
00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:06.292
The organization's purpose may be to schedule a meeting.

40
00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:09.848
The person's task may be to understand why the

41
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:13.714
meeting matters, consider their choices, arrange the support they

42
00:02:13.737 --> 00:02:16.048
want, and respond in a way they can use.

43
00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:19.568
Pause here if you would like to choose your

44
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.368
example. You can change it later.

45
00:02:24.417 --> 00:02:28.394
Standard one is the foundation. In practical terms, it

46
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:31.239
asks us to provide quality services that people can

47
00:02:31.261 --> 00:02:34.372
understand, that respect them, and that respond to their

48
00:02:34.394 --> 00:02:38.839
cultural beliefs, language, health literacy and other communication needs.

49
00:02:39.328 --> 00:02:42.417
This explanation is a guide to application. The original

50
00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:44.328
wording is available beside the lesson.

51
00:02:45.094 --> 00:02:48.450
Notice that the focus is the person's experience of

52
00:02:48.472 --> 00:02:52.272
the service, not simply whether we sent information. A

53
00:02:52.294 --> 00:02:54.717
letter can be accurate and still be difficult to

54
00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:57.828
use. An interpreter can be scheduled and still arrive

55
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.272
too late for meaningful participation. A form can be

56
00:03:01.294 --> 00:03:04.406
online and still be inaccessible to someone who uses

57
00:03:04.428 --> 00:03:08.494
assistive technology or does not have reliable internet access.

58
00:03:09.173 --> 00:03:12.107
Equity asks us to consider what people need to

59
00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:16.240
participate meaningfully. Giving everyone the same format does not

60
00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.684
tell us whether everyone can use it. At the

61
00:03:18.707 --> 00:03:20.951
same time, we should not assume a person's needs

62
00:03:20.973 --> 00:03:25.729
from their name, appearance, disability, language or community membership.

63
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.040
Ask respectfully, offer choices within the available process, and

64
00:03:30.062 --> 00:03:31.773
confirm whether the response works.

65
00:03:32.873 --> 00:03:36.273
There are fifteen standards. Standard one states the central

66
00:03:36.296 --> 00:03:40.384
purpose. Standards two through four address governance, leadership and

67
00:03:40.407 --> 00:03:44.407
workforce. Standards five through eight address communication and language

68
00:03:44.429 --> 00:03:48.851
assistance. Standards nine through fifteen address engagement, improvement and

69
00:03:48.873 --> 00:03:49.429
accountability.

70
00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:54.507
These are connected responsibilities. A staff member may recognize

71
00:03:54.529 --> 00:03:56.707
that a notice needs a clear offer of language

72
00:03:56.729 --> 00:04:00.662
assistance. Making that offer dependable may also require an

73
00:04:00.684 --> 00:04:05.484
approved process, trained staff, resources, community input and a

74
00:04:05.507 --> 00:04:09.018
way to monitor whether assistance actually reaches people. That

75
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.729
is why we will move between everyday interactions and

76
00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:14.129
the organizational conditions that support them.

77
00:04:14.673 --> 00:04:19.162
You are not expected to personally perform every organizational

78
00:04:19.807 --> 00:04:22.651
responsibility. Part of good practice is recognizing the difference

79
00:04:22.673 --> 00:04:24.918
between a change within your authority and an issue

80
00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:28.384
that needs coordination or approval. We will return to

81
00:04:28.407 --> 00:04:30.051
that distinction throughout the curriculum.

82
00:04:31.244 --> 00:04:34.978
Consider the sample notice. The team wants to improve

83
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.467
response rates. One suggestion is to send the same

84
00:04:38.489 --> 00:04:41.578
notice more often. Another is to ask how people

85
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.156
experience the notice and examine whether its instructions and

86
00:04:45.178 --> 00:04:48.378
assistance options are usable. Which approach gives you a

87
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.644
better starting point for understanding the barrier?

88
00:04:51.352 --> 00:04:54.397
Pause and make your choice in the activity below.

89
00:04:55.041 --> 00:04:58.241
The feedback will explain the reasoning. You can revise

90
00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:00.752
your answer; the purpose is to practice.

91
00:05:02.762 --> 00:05:06.451
Sending a reminder may eventually help, but repeating an

92
00:05:06.473 --> 00:05:09.918
unclear message can repeat the same barrier. Start by

93
00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.251
understanding the experience. That does not mean delaying every

94
00:05:13.273 --> 00:05:16.740
action until you have perfect information. It means making

95
00:05:16.762 --> 00:05:20.051
a reasonable inquiry, trying an appropriate change, and checking

96
00:05:20.073 --> 00:05:20.807
what happened.

97
00:05:21.929 --> 00:05:25.418
Finish this lesson with three short notes: the work

98
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.129
example you chose, the person or people who need

99
00:05:28.151 --> 00:05:31.462
to use it, and what successful participation would look

100
00:05:31.484 --> 00:05:35.440
like from their perspective. Keep these notes general and

101
00:05:35.462 --> 00:05:36.907
free of personal information.

102
00:05:37.728 --> 00:05:40.550
In the next lesson, we will look more closely

103
00:05:40.572 --> 00:05:43.861
at the difference between an observation and an assumption.

104
00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:48.217
That distinction helps us ask better questions and choose

105
00:05:48.239 --> 00:05:52.350
a useful response. For now, remember this starting point:

106
00:05:52.683 --> 00:05:55.683
a process is not fully understood by looking only

107
00:05:55.706 --> 00:05:58.661
at what we send or what we intend. We

108
00:05:58.683 --> 00:06:01.283
also need to learn how people encounter and use

109
00:06:01.306 --> 00:06:01.550
it.
